5. What I Understood
Tokenization: The process of breaking input text into smaller units called tokens.
Tokens: A token can be a complete word, part of a word, punctuation mark or character-related unit.
Token IDs: Each token is mapped to a numerical ID that a language model can process.
BPE: Builds subword units by repeatedly merging frequently occurring adjacent symbol pairs.
WordPiece: Uses a learned vocabulary and splits words into smaller pieces when the complete word is not available.
SentencePiece: A language-independent framework that learns subword units directly from raw text.
Token Count: Different tokenizers can produce different numbers of tokens for the same input text.

6. Implementation and Execution

I used three pretrained tokenizers:
BPE – GPT-2 (gpt2)
WordPiece – Multilingual BERT (bert-base-multilingual-cased)
SentencePiece – XLM-R (xlm-roberta-base)
I installed the required libraries using:
pip install -r requirements.txt
I executed the program using:
python tokenizer_comparison.py
The program loaded the three pretrained tokenizers and processed English, Telugu and Hindi sentences. It displayed the tokens, token IDs and token counts for each language.
